<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kozelsk</h1><p class="st-meta">1235 CE – 1494 CE (approx.) · capital Kozelsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny appanage of the Chernigov lands whose town held out against the Mongols for seven weeks in 1238.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kozelsk on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhizdra-river">Zhizdra</a> river, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a>, was a small town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> land, first mentioned in 1146. It became the seat of a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> in the early 13th century under a junior branch of the Olgovichi princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was small and poor, but its fortified town became famous in 1238 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a>'s army, returning from the north, besieged it for seven weeks. The defenders, led in the name of the child prince Vasily, killed many attackers before the town fell and everyone was slaughtered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> reportedly called it the evil town.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rebuilt, Kozelsk passed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in the 14th century and its princes became Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. In the wars of the late 15th century it changed sides, and the peace of 1494 confirmed it as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1235 CE – 1494 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.28%;width:5.15%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1235 CE – 1494 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kozelsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasily of Kozelsk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kozelsk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=principality-of-kozelsk" aria-label="Principality of Kozelsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5472 324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kozelsk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5792 580L5724 651M5792 580L5556 562M5792 580L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5724 651h0M5556 562h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5792 580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5792 580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kozelsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1364&amp;focus=principality-of-kozelsk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
